#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "2aca2611-5903-5a92-8409-8fe3af32614a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5827" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(2x+1)(x-2)(x-7) > 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2x+1)(x-2)(x-7)\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0$.\n\nThe solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n$x=2$, and $x=7$. These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right), \\quad \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},2 \\right), \\quad (2,7), \\quad (7, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac12\\right)$ | $\\left(-\\dfrac12,2\\right)$ | $(2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-7$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)> 0$, we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(7,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) > 0$ for all\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right) \\cup (7,\\infty)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "2cdfee85-e9c7-5e91-bbef-16daaaaf1d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-5828" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x^4 + 2x^3 - 3x^2 \\leq 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} + 2x^{3} - 3x^{2} &= x^{2}(x^{2} + 2x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x^{2} + 3x - x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x(x + 3) - (x + 3)) \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x + 3)(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2(x+3)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=-3$, $x=0$ and $x=1$. These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n$(-\\infty,-3), \\quad (-3,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x)\\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-3, 0)$ and $(0,1)$.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that the solution to\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx \\in [-3,0]\\cup [0,1] = [-3,1]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"}] :question/id #uuid "bad47d1a-817e-529d-8eb5-7a2ab446370a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5835" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(2x^2-3) < 5x$." :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(2x^{2} - 3) & < 5x \\\\ x(2x^{2} - 3) - 5x & < 0 \\\\ x(2x^{2} - 3 - 5) & < 0 \\\\ x(2x^{2} - 8) & < 0 \\\\ 2x(x^{2} - 4) & < 0 \\\\ 2x(x + 2)(x - 2) & < 0 \\\\ x(x + 2)(x - 2) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) < 0$.\n\nThe solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=-2$, $x = 0$, and $x = 2$. These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n$(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,0),\\quad (0,2),\\quad (2,\\infty)$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0$, we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(0,2)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "cc631d97-d7f8-5798-8dff-f816e63bbf44" :question/math-academy-id "e-7755" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(x+2)^2(x-1) \\leq 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)^2(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nThe solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=1$. These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n$(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+2)^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(0,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in \\{-2\\} \\cup[0, 1]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d27313b-580f-50d6-82df-023fe3492322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131697} {:answer/id #uuid "c095e2e3-b320-568a-99b8-c49fc2ac8f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131698} {:answer/id #uuid "c221ba55-0d66-5df0-8584-7b3cf4a3fa82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-1)∪(1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131699} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfbf121-2ec5-5587-9459-3771dff4d7a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (- ∞,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131700} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5e9fb4-c2a4-5188-9803-997171ad889f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (1, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d27313b-580f-50d6-82df-023fe3492322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x ∈ (-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131697} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccdafc97-7691-5a17-9c10-c7b1794cc870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131696}] :question/id #uuid "d7c7012e-7230-5abb-8c48-85a5afa58965" :question/math-academy-id "q-40367" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x^{4} - 1 < 0$."}]}
